          <h2>American Girl Magazine e-cards weren&rsquo;t preserved.</h2>
          <p>This category was server-rendered from a database that no longer exists, and the page itself was never reached by any web crawler during the 2008 era. Every other E-Card Central category was — but a magazine-themed dropdown click would have to <em>happen</em> for an archive bot to follow it, and apparently nobody did.</p>

          <div class="sources">
            <strong>Sources checked — all empty for this URL in 2008</strong>
            <ul>
              <li>Internet Archive Wayback Machine — full CDX (every capture, every year). Two captures total, both 2011&ndash;2012, after Mattel redesigned the site and dropped this category entirely.</li>
              <li>Common Crawl indexes 2008&ndash;2015 — the agm dropdown URL is not present in any.</li>
              <li>Memento Time Travel aggregator — service unresolvable (host down).</li>
              <li>archive.today / archive.ph — no captures.</li>
              <li>Google / Bing / Yandex caches and search results — no fan-site preservation, no Tumblr / Pinterest / DeviantArt mirrors, no AmericanGirlFan blog backup.</li>
              <li>American Girl Wiki + Fandom — no e-card screenshots from this category.</li>
              <li>The /agmg/ (magazine) subsite itself — linked only to <a href="/ecards/index.php">E-Card Central</a> as a whole, never to category=agm.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="ask">
            <em>If you have ANY copy</em> &mdash; an old saved HTML page, a school computer&rsquo;s browser cache, a screenshot, a SWF file with <code>agm_</code> in the name, even just remembering specific cards by name &mdash; please reach out. Restoring this would close one of the last real gaps in the 2008 American Girl preservation effort and would matter to the entire AG community.
          </div>

